  {q:"How does the fossil record of dinosaur nesting behavior compare to modern archosaur nesting strategies?",correct:"Evidence of nest guarding, colonial nesting, and brooding posture in dinosaur nests closely parallels behaviors seen in both modern birds and crocodilians today",options:["No comparison is possible","Evidence of nest guarding, colonial nesting, and brooding posture in dinosaur nests closely parallels behaviors seen in both modern birds and crocodilians today","Only birds share these behaviors","Dinosaur nesting was completely unique"],fact:"Oviraptorosaur fossils found brooding on nests in the exact posture modern birds use to incubate eggs is one of the most compelling links between dinosaurs and modern avian behavior — the posture is unmistakably the same!"},
  {q:"What is the significance of recently discovered polar dinosaur faunas from Alaska's Prince Creek Formation?",correct:"Rich dinosaur faunas from Arctic Alaska show dinosaurs lived through polar winters with months of darkness, suggesting either migration or cold-weather adaptations unlike modern reptiles",options:["No polar dinosaurs have been found","Rich dinosaur faunas from Arctic Alaska show dinosaurs lived through months of darkness, suggesting either migration or cold-weather adaptations unlike modern reptiles","Only small dinosaurs lived in the Arctic","Polar dinosaurs were always warm-blooded mammals"],fact:"Nanuqsaurus ('polar bear lizard') — a small tyrannosaur from Arctic Alaska — shows that even apex predators adapted to polar conditions. The polar fauna is as diverse as lower-latitude ones, suggesting dinosaurs were far more ecologically flexible than once thought!"}
